│ [CAND] overfit_not_settled_statically       Implementation Overfit
│        THREE STEPS, IN THIS ORDER - REPORT, ASK TO INSPECT, THEN RULE. (1)
│        REPORT every finding below to the CB in plain words: what the test
│        pins, and which other correct implementation it would reject. Never
│        paste an id as if it explained itself. (2) ASK THE CB WHETHER TO
│        INSPECT, and WAIT for the answer. Do not investigate unasked and do
│        not rule unasked - this check reads test source only, so every
│        finding here is a QUESTION, and the CB decides whether it is worth
│        your time to chase. Asking them to adjudicate it themselves is the
│        other failure: they should be choosing whether you dig, not doing the
│        digging. (3) IF THEY SAY YES, inspect with TEST_FITNESS.md - read the
│        test BODY rather than the diff (the diff hides the helpers) to see
│        whether a symbol is asserted BY NAME or discovered by behaviour; grep
│        the BASE tree to check the "the golden fix introduces this" claim,
│        the single most common false finding here; open the posters; and
│        MEASURE where the read runs out. What the evidence then shows to be a
│        FALSE POSITIVE you clear YOURSELF, with that evidence as the reason -
│        it is recorded and replays on every later audit, so the gate stays
│        green without anyone re-typing anything. What the evidence shows to
│        be REAL you report as a defect to fix, not something to clear. Where
│        it stays inconclusive, say so and go back to the CB. NEVER clear one
│        you have not inspected: that turns a blocker into a formality and the
│        CB never learns their tests were questioned. HOW THIS DIMENSION
│        WORKS. Every overfit finding above BLOCKS - no QC pass is recorded,
│        so oracle and nop stay grounded until each one is ruled on. But each
│        is a heuristic read off test source, not a proven defect: this check
│        cannot see the REPOSITORY, where the rubric's "plausible
│        implementation has precedent in the repo" is decided, nor the
│        POSTERS, where a literal that looks golden-only is often the spec. So
│        each is a QUESTION, answered in either direction with a mandatory
│        reason: `--adjudicated <id>=cleared:"why"` drops it to OK,
│        `=confirmed:"why"` promotes it to a real defect to fix. DO NOT loosen
│        an assertion merely because it is listed - on a well-built task most
│        of these clear, and weakening a good test is the more expensive
│        mistake. TEST_FITNESS.md is not reserved for a contested ruling - it
│        is how you FORM one: the investigation: the digest, both posters, and
│        a container where the alternative implementation can be RUN in about
│        two seconds, which settles it as evidence instead of opinion.
│        UNDERFIT - a plausible wrong fix that still scores - is not reachable
│        here at all and needs that same procedure.
